    "revision": 56,
    "objective": "Allow exact USER-granted hosted PR adoption when external write is declared but withheld from semantic agents\n\nFix the generic mismatch between existing PR adoption admission and task execution routing: branch_pr tasks declare external_write for operator-owned provider adoption, while semantic WorkOrders correctly forbid that effect. Keep semantic authority unchanged; require the declaration, network-read authority, approved plan, canonical task, and the existing exact USER operation grant. Add a regression test using a real resolved execution contract. This unblocks native adoption of Dependabot PRs #6944 and #6935 for 0.7.13.",
    "acceptance_criteria": [
      "- In scope: Fix the generic mismatch between existing PR adoption admission and task execution routing: branch_pr tasks declare external_write for operator-owned provider adoption, while semantic WorkOrders correctly forbid that effect. Keep semantic authority unchanged; require the declaration, network-read authority, approved plan, canonical task, and the existing exact USER operation grant. Add a regression test using a real resolved execution contract. This unblocks native adoption of Dependabot PRs #6944 and #6935 for 0.7.13.\n- Out of scope: unrelated refactors not required for \"Allow exact USER-granted hosted PR adoption when external write is declared but withheld from semantic agents\".",
      "1. Execute approved WorkItem admit-declared-operator-adoption.\n2. Execute approved WorkItem reject-semantic-external-write-adoption.",
      "PLANNER fallback scaffold. Replace with task-specific acceptance checks when PLANNER context is available.\n\n1. Review the changed artifact or behavior for the `code` task. Expected: the requested outcome is visible and matches the approved scope.\n2. Run the most relevant validation step for the `code` task. Expected: it succeeds without unexpected regressions in touched scope.\n3. Compare the final result against the task summary and scope. Expected: any remaining follow-up is explicit in ## Findings."
    ]
  },
